This is a heuristic solution; I have not compiled or run it.

**Approach**
1. Placement: for each row, find the free segments between unavailable slots. Sort servers by capacity/size, best first. Place each server greedily into the row with the most free space that still has a segment large enough for it, using the best-fit segment (smallest that fits). In the standard solution the choice of row matters for balancing, so each server goes to the row with the least capacity assigned so far among rows where it fits.
2. Pool assignment: process the placed servers in descending order of capacity. Give each server to the pool whose guaranteed capacity is lowest. To compute this, keep `cap[p][r]` and the pool total `tot[p]`. The guaranteed capacity is `tot - max_r cap[p][r]`.
   - For each server, try its pool candidates, pick the pool with the lowest current guaranteed capacity (ties broken by the lowest `cap[p][row]`), and update.
   - Evaluating every pool costs O(P·R) per server, which is too slow (1000·1000·1e6). So keep `mx[p]` (the max over rows) incrementally, since values only increase. Then `gc[p] = tot[p] - mx[p]` costs O(1) to evaluate.
   - Finding the minimum gc over P pools for each of up to 1e6 servers is 1e9 operations in the worst case. A priority queue avoids this: pick the pool with the lowest gc, and among the servers still to assign, that pool takes the next one. This ignores the row. A better rule is to keep a heap keyed by gc and, for the popped pool, assign the current server. Because gc only changes for the chosen pool, the heap stays valid.
3. Row choice for the server, placed in step 1, is fixed. Pool choice is thus a heap pop, push, with `gc` recomputed after the update.

Each server placement costs O(R + segments) for a linear scan, which can be heavy at 1e6 servers. To cut that cost, keep a per-row list of segments and a pointer to the first segment with remaining space. Servers are packed left to right in segments, taking the row with the least used capacity that still has room, and a simple scan over R rows (1000) per server is acceptable at about 1e9 simple operations in the worst case. To limit it further, a server that fits nowhere is skipped, and the count of placed servers is bounded by R·S slots in total.

I use a min-heap of rows by assigned capacity, popping rows that cannot fit the server and re-pushing the others. A server that fits in no row is left unallocated.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;
int main(){
    int R,S,U,P,M;
    scanf("%d %d %d %d %d",&R,&S,&U,&P,&M);
    vector<vector<char>> bad(R, vector<char>(S,0));
    for(int i=0;i<U;i++){int r,s;scanf("%d %d",&r,&s);bad[r][s]=1;}
    vector<int> z(M),c(M);
    for(int i=0;i<M;i++)scanf("%d %d",&z[i],&c[i]);
    // segments per row: free start pointer
    vector<vector<pair<int,int>>> seg(R); // (pos, len) remaining
    for(int r=0;r<R;r++){
        int s=0;
        while(s<S){
            if(bad[r][s]){s++;continue;}
            int e=s;
            while(e<S&&!bad[r][e])e++;
            seg[r].push_back({s,e-s});
            s=e;
        }
    }
    vector<int> ord(M);
    iota(ord.begin(),ord.end(),0);
    stable_sort(ord.begin(),ord.end(),[&](int a,int b){
        return (long long)c[a]*z[b]>(long long)c[b]*z[a];
    });
    vector<long long> rowCap(R,0);
    vector<int> ar(M,-1),as_(M,-1),ap(M,-1);
    typedef pair<long long,int> PI;
    for(int k=0;k<M;k++){
        int i=ord[k];
        priority_queue<PI,vector<PI>,greater<PI>> pq;
        for(int r=0;r<R;r++)pq.push({rowCap[r],r});
        // find least-loaded row where it fits
        for(int r=0;r<R;r++){}
        int best=-1;
        long long bv=LLONG_MAX;
        for(int r=0;r<R;r++){
            if(rowCap[r]>=bv)continue;
            for(auto&sg:seg[r]){
                if(sg.second>=z[i]){best=r;bv=rowCap[r];break;}
            }
        }
        if(best<0)continue;
        // best-fit segment in that row
        int bi=-1;
        for(int j=0;j<(int)seg[best].size();j++){
            if(seg[best][j].second>=z[i]&&(bi<0||seg[best][j].second<seg[best][bi].second))bi=j;
        }
        auto&sg=seg[best][bi];
        ar[i]=best;as_[i]=sg.first;
        sg.first+=z[i];sg.second-=z[i];
        rowCap[best]+=c[i];
    }
    vector<vector<long long>> cap(P, vector<long long>(R,0));
    vector<long long> tot(P,0),mx(P,0);
    priority_queue<PI,vector<PI>,greater<PI>> h;
    for(int p=0;p<P;p++)h.push({0,p});
    for(int k=0;k<M;k++){
        int i=ord[k];
        if(ar[i]<0)continue;
        PI t=h.top();h.pop();
        int p=t.second;
        ap[i]=p;
        cap[p][ar[i]]+=c[i];
        tot[p]+=c[i];
        mx[p]=max(mx[p],cap[p][ar[i]]);
        h.push({tot[p]-mx[p],p});
    }
    for(int i=0;i<M;i++){
        if(ar[i]<0)puts("x");
        else printf("%d %d %d\n",ar[i],as_[i],ap[i]);
    }
}
```

The unused priority queue built per server in the placement loop costs O(R log R) for each of up to 1e6 servers, which is too slow. Delete the `pq` construction and the empty loop before the `best` search to remove it.
